obligations beyond this file.

Where binary attribution actually lives
---------------------------------------

The three Java services are packaged by spring-boot-maven-plugin as repackaged
("fat") jars. Dependency jars are placed under BOOT-INF/lib/ inside that jar,
each retaining its own META-INF/LICENSE and META-INF/NOTICE. The plugin does
not aggregate them, so the fat jar's own top-level META-INF contains no combined
NOTICE. The container images (deployment/Dockerfile.control-plane,
Dockerfile.worker, Dockerfile.search-service) COPY only that jar out of the
builder stage, so the same is true inside the images: dependency licences travel
with the nested jars, not in a top-level aggregate.

Anyone redistributing a binary is responsible for assembling the aggregated
attribution that Apache-2.0 section 4(d) requires from those nested jars. This
file deliberately does not enumerate a handful of dependencies: the runtime
closure includes roughly fifteen non-Apache-licensed artifacts (permissive or
dual-licensed: MIT, BSD, CDDL/EPL), reached mostly transitively through Hadoop,
and a partial list would read as a complete one.

Licence review as of this commit
--------------------------------

The declared dependencies are Apache-2.0 (Iceberg, Hadoop, Parquet, Lucene,
Spring Boot, Flyway, MapStruct, Jackson, AWS SDK for Java v2), MIT (SLF4J API,
Lombok, Testcontainers), BSD-2-Clause (PostgreSQL JDBC driver) and EPL-2.0
(JUnit 5, test scope). Transitives brought in by hadoop-common add BSD
(protobuf-java, jsch, dnsjava, and re2j under the BSD-style "Go License"),
Apache-2.0-or-EPL-1.0 (Jetty 9.4) and
CDDL-1.1-or-GPL-2.0-with-classpath-exception dual licences (javax.servlet-api,
jersey 1.x, jsp-api) — for which the CDDL option is taken. No dependency is
offered under GPL, LGPL or AGPL only.
